\paragraph{(b) Per-test precision and recall.}
At the per-test level (seed 42), \cls{} achieves accuracy 87.51\%, fail \fone{} 87.86\% (precision 82.75\%, recall 93.64\%), and pass \fone{} 87.15\% (precision 93.24\%, recall 81.80\%). Of 114 problems in the test set, 48 (42.1\%) are perfectly predicted across all their tests. Performance varies by mutation type: \texttt{return\_value} is easiest (97.3\% accuracy) while \texttt{off\_by\_one} is hardest (82.6\%). Multi-F2P instances with 6--10 tests reach 96.3\% per-test accuracy, corroborating the oracle analysis finding that per-test granularity is most valuable when multiple F2P tests are available.

\paragraph{(c) Majority voting.}
Majority voting ($K = 3$, temperature 0.7) decreases accuracy from 87.51\% to 86.83\% ($-$0.68\pp{}). Of 127 split-vote samples, 28 flip from correct to incorrect while only 15 flip in the favorable direction (net loss: 13 samples). The model's errors are systematic rather than stochastic, so sampling-based ensembles do not help; single-pass greedy decoding remains optimal.

